  "text": "Every septic company with a big marketing budget wants a bigger service area. We just made one smaller. Six weeks ago, our septic client trusted us with a really big Google Ads budget. Today, they told me they're buying two new pump trucks. My septic client is growing fast. My septic client is growing so fast they need two new pump trucks. Naturally, we shrink their map. Big ad budget for your septic company. Your service area might be too big, and here's why. We made one change today that sounds backwards for this busy septic company. Six weeks in, my client is buying two new pump trucks, and today we made their service area smaller. I know. Sounds backwards. Here's why. They have a big ad budget, and when you spread a big budget across a big map, your ads show up everywhere, including the far edge. The towns you don't want to send a truck to and pay for fuel for. You pay for that click and then you pay for the drive, so we pull their mapping closer. Same budget, now it all lands near home where the jobs are close and the trucks stay busy. Will it work? We're watching it closely and I'll tell you how it goes. But here's what I want you to hear. Spending more on ads doesn't mean you have to reach further. Sometimes it means aiming better. If your ads bring calls from places you don't want to drive, that's the first thing I look at. Send me a message.",
